Refs in conditional / branching control flow
============================================

A `&T` declared inside a branch must be dropped at the
branch's scope exit, NOT at the enclosing function's exit.
If the branch is skipped, the ref is never constructed and
must not be (double-)decremented.

Bug families to surface:

  - **Lexical decref of skipped branch**: emitting a decref
    at function exit for a ref declared only in the `then`
    branch, regardless of whether the branch was taken.
    Decrements a null pointer (best case) or random heap
    memory (worst).
  - **Missed decref in taken branch**: if the branch
    cleanup is wired to the enclosing function rather than
    the branch's local scope, the ref might leak.
  - **Double-drop on early-return path**: a branch with an
    early return must decref locally; the function-exit
    pass must not re-decref the same already-dropped ref.

Companion to `defer_skipped_branch.lsysl` (which pins defer
semantics in skipped branches); this file pins **refcount**
semantics under the same control-flow shapes.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct RcdBox
        v: int

Ref declared in `then` branch — drop is branch-local
----------------------------------------------------

When the branch fires, the ref is constructed (rc=1) and
must drop to 0 at the branch's scope exit (before the
following statement). When the branch is skipped, no ref
exists and the function-exit cleanup must not act.

To observe both cases without leaking the box, we read
`v` while the branch is active and write to a `*int`
counter that we read after the function returns.

    rcd_branch_only(take: bool, out: *int) -> unit
        if take
            val r: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(100)
            *out = r.v
        // After this point, r is gone (taken case) or never existed (skipped).

    #test
    test_ref_in_then_branch_taken() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_branch_only(true, &x)
        assert(x == 100, s"expected 100, got $x")

    #test
    test_ref_in_then_branch_skipped() -> unit
        var x = -1
        rcd_branch_only(false, &x)
        // Branch never ran; out untouched.
        assert(x == -1, s"expected -1 (untouched), got $x")

If-else where each branch declares its own ref
----------------------------------------------

Both arms construct an independent ref. Each arm's ref is
local to that arm and must drop at the arm's scope exit.
Pins that the ARC pass tracks per-branch lifetimes
separately (no "merge into the fn scope and decr at exit").

    rcd_either_arm(pick: int, out: *int) -> unit
        if pick == 1
            val a: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(11)
            *out = a.v
        else
            val b: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(22)
            *out = b.v

    #test
    test_either_arm_then_taken() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_either_arm(1, &x)
        assert(x == 11, s"expected 11, got $x")

    #test
    test_either_arm_else_taken() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_either_arm(7, &x)
        assert(x == 22, s"expected 22, got $x")

Early-return from a branch with a live ref
------------------------------------------

The branch constructs a ref, then returns from the
function. The branch-scoped cleanup must fire BEFORE the
return (or the return path must include the decref).
Without it, the box leaks.

The test runs the function many times; on any backend that
leaks per-call, the allocator's working set grows. We
can't observe leaks directly, but we can confirm that
N invocations all return the right value (which they
wouldn't if the heap was corrupted by mismatched decrefs).

    rcd_early_return_in_branch(x: int) -> int
        if x > 0
            val r: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(x * 2)
            return r.v
        return 0

    #test
    test_early_return_in_branch_pos() -> unit
        assert(rcd_early_return_in_branch(5) == 10, s"5*2 = 10")

    #test
    test_early_return_in_branch_zero() -> unit
        assert(rcd_early_return_in_branch(0) == 0, s"zero case")

    #test
    test_many_early_returns() -> unit
        var i = 0
        var sum = 0
        while i < 100
            sum = sum + rcd_early_return_in_branch(i)
            i = i + 1
        // sum = 2 * (1+2+...+99) = 2 * 4950 = 9900
        assert(sum == 9900, s"expected 9900, got $sum")

Ref reassigned inside a branch — old binding drops
---------------------------------------------------

A ref is constructed before the branch, then conditionally
reassigned inside the branch. The old binding's ref must
drop at reassignment (rc-- → maybe free), and the new
binding's ref takes over the slot. After the branch, the
binding holds the new ref (taken case) or the old one
(skipped case).

    rcd_reassign_in_branch(take: bool) -> int
        var r: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(1)
        if take
            r = new RcdBox(2)
        return r.v

    #test
    test_reassign_in_branch_taken() -> unit
        val v = rcd_reassign_in_branch(true)
        assert(v == 2, s"taken: new ref's value, got $v")

    #test
    test_reassign_in_branch_skipped() -> unit
        val v = rcd_reassign_in_branch(false)
        assert(v == 1, s"skipped: original ref's value, got $v")

Nested branches with refs at each level
---------------------------------------

Three nested `if`s, each declaring its own ref. Each layer's
ref must drop at its own scope exit. The deepest active
branch's ref is the one read out.

    rcd_nested(a: bool, b: bool, c: bool, out: *int) -> unit
        if a
            val ra: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(1)
            if b
                val rb: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(10)
                if c
                    val rc: &RcdBox = new RcdBox(100)
                    *out = ra.v + rb.v + rc.v
                else
                    *out = ra.v + rb.v
            else
                *out = ra.v

    #test
    test_nested_aaa() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_nested(true, true, true, &x)
        assert(x == 111, s"a+b+c = 111, got $x")

    #test
    test_nested_aab() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_nested(true, true, false, &x)
        assert(x == 11, s"a+b = 11, got $x")

    #test
    test_nested_a_only() -> unit
        var x = 0
        rcd_nested(true, false, false, &x)
        assert(x == 1, s"a only = 1, got $x")

    #test
    test_nested_none() -> unit
        var x = 999
        rcd_nested(false, false, false, &x)
        assert(x == 999, s"no branch taken, out untouched, got $x")
